{
  "dom_id": "step-q362547",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Compute the arc length $L$ of $y=\\frac{2}{3}(x+2)^{3/2}$ for $x\\in [1,6].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$ \n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{\u2032}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}(x+2)^{3/2},$ $a=1,$ and $b=6.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(x+2)^{1/2}=\\sqrt{x+2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{\u2032}(x)]^{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{\u2032}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[\\sqrt{x+2}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{\u2032}(x)]^{2}}=\\sqrt{x+3}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{6}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{\u2032}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{6}\\sqrt{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{3}(x+3)^{3/2})|}_{1}^{6} \\\\  & =[\\frac{2}{3}(6+3)^{3/2}]-[\\frac{2}{3}(1+3)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{54}{3}-\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{38}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
